    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Korean kingdom founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a> in 918 that reunited the peninsula in 936 and ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a> (Kaesong).</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was the state religion: the court carved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana">Tripitaka Koreana</a>, and Goryeo potters perfected inlaid celadon.</li><li>Military rule from 1170 and Mongol overlordship from 1259 weakened the crown, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Yi Seong-gye</a> replaced it with Joseon in 1392.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jikji">Jikji</a>, printed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungdeoksa">Heungdeoksa</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheongju">Cheongju</a> in 1377, is the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type; its only surviving volume is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheque-nationale-de-france">National Library of France</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

