    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gung-ye.jpg" alt="Gung Ye" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EA%B6%81%EC%98%88.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">(Wikibotbot) 위키봇봇, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goryeo grew out of the collapse of Unified Silla in the late ninth century, when regional strongmen divided the peninsula into the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a>, the son of a maritime trading family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Songak</a> on the west coast, rose as a general and naval commander in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a>, ruler of Taebong. In 918 a group of generals overthrew the increasingly erratic Gung Ye and raised Wang Geon to the throne. He named his kingdom Goryeo, a shortened form of Goguryeo, to claim the heritage of the old northern kingdom, and in 919 moved the capital to his home town, renamed Kaegyong (modern Kaesong). As King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goguryeo">Taejo</a> (r. 918–943) he pursued a patient policy of alliances, marrying into some twenty-nine powerful families and winning over local lords with titles, land and the royal surname. Silla's last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongsun-of-silla">Gyeongsun</a>, surrendered peacefully in 935, and in 936 Taejo defeated Later Baekje at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-illicheon">Battle of Illicheon</a>, reuniting the peninsula. He also welcomed refugees from Balhae, destroyed by the Khitan in 926, among them its crown prince, and presented Goryeo as the heir of all the earlier Korean kingdoms. The Ten Injunctions attributed to him, a political testament for his successors, urged Buddhist patronage and warned against adopting Khitan customs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kaesong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaesong.jpg" alt="Kaesong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koryo_Songgyungwan_University.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">魯班, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goryeo society was dominated by a capital-based aristocracy of a few great lineages, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongwon-yi-clan">Gyeongwon Yi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haeju-choe-clan">Haeju Choe</a>, which intermarried with the royal house and monopolised high office. Below them stood the hyangni, hereditary local functionaries who ran the counties, then free commoners who farmed and paid taxes, and at the bottom slaves and the inhabitants of special districts (<em>hyang</em>, <em>so</em> and <em>bugok</em>) of inferior status. Officials and soldiers were supported by the stipend land system (<em>jeonsigwa</em>), first introduced in 976, which granted the right to collect rent from land rather than its ownership. Women had more standing than in later centuries: daughters inherited property equally with sons, and husbands often lived in their wives' households. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was the state religion. Great monasteries owned land and slaves, the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uicheon">Uicheon</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheontae">Cheontae</a> school, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinul">Jinul</a> reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seon-of-balhae">Seon</a> (Zen) Buddhism, and the state festivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeondeunghoe">Yeondeunghoe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palgwanhoe">Palgwanhoe</a> combined Buddhist ritual with native cults. Foreign trade passed through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byeongnando">Byeongnando</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a>, visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Chinese, Japanese, Khitan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> traders and, in the eleventh century, by Arab merchants, through whom the name Goryeo probably travelled west to become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Potters perfected jade-green celadon, admired by the Song envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-jing">Xu Jing</a> in 1123, and developed inlaid (<em>sanggam</em>) decoration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gang-gam-chan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gang-gam-chan.jpg" alt="Gang Gam-chan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gang-gam-chan">Gang Gam-chan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EA%B0%95%EA%B0%90%EC%B0%AC_%EC%9E%A5%EA%B5%B0_%ED%9D%89%EC%83%81.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">공공누리, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goryeo maintained a standing central army of two guards and six divisions in the capital, supported by provincial forces and by garrisons in the two northern border regions, the Bukgye and Donggye, which remained under military administration. Its greatest early test came from the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. In 993 a Khitan army invaded, but the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seo-hui">Seo Hui</a> negotiated its withdrawal and secured the land east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu</a> that became six garrison districts. A second invasion in 1010 burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a> and forced King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyeonjong-of-goryeo">Hyeonjong</a> to flee south. When the Khitan returned in 1018, the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-gam-chan">Gang Gam-chan</a> destroyed their army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gwiju">Battle of Gwiju</a> in 1019; according to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goryeosa">Goryeosa</a></em> only a few thousand of the invaders escaped. Goryeo then built a long wall across its northern frontier from the Yalu to the east coast, completed in 1044. In 1107 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yun-gwan">Yun Gwan</a> led a special force, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byeolmuban">Byeolmuban</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> and built nine forts in the northeast, but they were handed back in 1109. The Mongol invasions from 1231 brought nearly three decades of devastation, resisted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a> and by local garrisons and peasant fighters. Later Goryeo supplied ships and troops for the failed Yuan invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1274 and 1281, and late in the dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-mu-seon">Choe Mu-seon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> office armed ships with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> that destroyed a Japanese pirate fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jinpo">Jinpo</a> in 1380.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/choe-chung-heon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choe-chung-heon.jpg" alt="Choe Chung-heon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/choe-chung-heon">Choe Chung-heon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokyo_National_Museum_Toyokan_(52504076964).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dick Thomas Johnson from Tokyo, Japan, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goryeo's kings governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a> through institutions modelled on Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song China</a> but adapted to an aristocratic society. The central government comprised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretariat-chancellery">Secretariat-Chancellery</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/department-of-state-affairs">Department of State Affairs</a> with its six ministries, and the Security Council, while senior officials met jointly in councils such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobyeongmasa">Dobyeongmasa</a>, which became the real centre of power late in the dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwangjong-of-goryeo">Gwangjong</a> (r. 949–975) strengthened the crown against the great families: his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-review-act">Slave Review Act</a> of 956 freed many people who had been wrongly enslaved, and in 958, on the advice of the Chinese official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ssang-gi">Ssang Gi</a>, he introduced civil service examinations. Seongjong (r. 981–997) adopted the Confucian programme urged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-seung-ro">Choe Seung-ro</a>, created twelve provincial governorships (<em>mok</em>) in 983 and founded the National Academy (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gukjagam">Gukjagam</a>) in 992. Examinations never displaced birth, for the protection privilege (<em>eum</em>) let sons of high officials enter office without them. Royal authority was shaken by the revolt of the in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-ja-gyeom">Yi Ja-gyeom</a> in 1126 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myocheong">Myocheong</a>'s rebellion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in 1135, suppressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-busik">Kim Bu-sik</a>. After a coup in 1170 military strongmen ruled; from 1196 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-chung-heon">Choe Chung-heon</a> and his descendants governed through their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyojeong-dogam">Directorate General for Policy Formulation</a> while kings reigned in name only, until 1258. Under Yuan overlordship Goryeo kings married Mongol princesses, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengdong-province">Yuan Branch Secretariat</a> supervised the court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi.jpg" alt="Samguk sagi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samguk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gomanari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goryeo left a lasting mark on Korean identity. By uniting the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> and absorbing refugees from Balhae, it fixed the idea of a single kingdom covering most of the peninsula, and its name, carried abroad by merchants, became the Western name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-busik">Kim Bu-sik</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a></em> of 1145, the oldest surviving Korean history, and the monk Iryeon's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a></em> of the 1280s have shaped how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> see their ancient past ever since. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana">Tripitaka Koreana</a>, the Buddhist canon carved on more than 81,000 woodblocks between 1236 and 1251 to win divine protection against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, replaced an earlier set burned by them in 1232; it is preserved at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haeinsa">Haeinsa</a> temple and recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a>. Goryeo printers also used movable metal type: the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jikji">Jikji</a></em>, a collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seon-buddhism">Seon Buddhist</a> teachings printed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungdeoksa">Heungdeoksa</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheongju">Cheongju</a> in 1377, is the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type, and literary sources record metal-type <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> in Goryeo earlier in the thirteenth century. Inlaid celadon is regarded as one of the summits of East Asian ceramics and was much imitated in later Korean wares. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>, which entered Goryeo under Yuan rule through scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-hyang">An Hyang</a>, produced the reform-minded officials who later founded Joseon, and Joseon kept much of Goryeo's administrative framework and its capital region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion.jpg" alt="Red Turban Rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Turban.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Goryeo's decline began with the Mongol invasions. After the first attack in 1231 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-regime">Choe regime</a> moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a> in 1232, safe behind its narrow straits from steppe cavalry, while the mainland was ravaged by repeated campaigns until 1259, when the court made peace. The court returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a> in 1270, provoking the revolt of the Sambyeolcho units, crushed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju</a> in 1273. For eight decades Goryeo kings were sons-in-law of the Yuan emperors, often lived in the Yuan capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> and could be deposed by them, while pro-Yuan families such as that of Empress Gi grew powerful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongmin-of-goryeo">King Gongmin</a> (r. 1351–1374) exploited the Yuan's weakness, purging the Gi family in 1356 and recovering the northeastern territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ssangseong-prefecture">Ssangseong</a>, and attempted land and slave reforms through the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-don">Sin Don</a>. But <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban</a> rebels invaded in 1359 and 1361, briefly taking Kaegyong, Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> ravaged the coasts, and Gongmin was murdered in 1374. When King U ordered an attack on the Ming in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> in 1388, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Yi Seong-gye</a> turned his army back at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wihwa-island">Wihwa Island</a>, seized power and, with reformist officials, carried out a sweeping land reform in 1391 that ruined the old aristocracy. The loyalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeong-mong-ju">Jeong Mong-ju</a> was assassinated in 1392, and that year Yi deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongyang-of-goryeo">Gongyang</a> and founded Joseon.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Goryeo reunited the Korean peninsula after the fall of Silla and ruled it for almost five centuries through an aristocratic bureaucracy and a Buddhist court, surviving Khitan and Mongol invasions and producing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana">Tripitaka Koreana</a>, inlaid celadon and the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type.</em></p>

